/**
@mainpage RVCARA

Retrieval-based voice conversion as an audio plug-in. Drop it on a vocal track and the vocal is
re-sung in the trained voice, offline, on the CPU, with nothing sent to a server. The voice
loads by itself; there is nothing to choose.

The model is RVC v2. Voices are trained and exported with the sibling RTVoice project; this
repository is the plug-in only.

@section modes The two ways it runs

RVC is not causal: its content encoder is a transformer over the whole utterance, its pitch
estimator sees the future, and its vocoder is conditioned on a pitch track gap-filled end to
end. Conversion is therefore always offline. What differs is how the plug-in gets the audio.

Under **ARA**, the host grants random access to the whole region up front, the model sees the
entire performance, and playback becomes a memory read. Region edits, per-region settings and
session persistence all work.

Loaded as an **ordinary insert**, rvcara::InsertConverter captures the audio against the host
timeline, converts it when the transport stops, and plays the conversion back in place on the
next pass. The first pass is dry.

Neither mode is usable for live monitoring.

@section layout Layout

Only the two JUCE entry points sit at the top of `src/`; everything else is grouped by layer,
and includes are path-qualified so a file's dependencies name their layer.

| Directory | Contents |
| --- | --- |
| `src/` | rvcara::Processor, rvcara::Editor |
| `src/common/` | rvcara::ConversionSettings, rvcara::PitchEdit, rvcara::BinaryMatrix |
| `src/dsp/` | rvcara::SincResampler, rvcara::ZeroPhaseFilter, rvcara::MelSpectrogram, pitch maths |
| `src/model/` | rvcara::VoiceModel, rvcara::ConversionEngine, rvcara::NoteSegmenter, rvcara::VoiceLoader, sessions, manifest |
| `src/ara/` | rvcara::DocumentController, rvcara::PlaybackRenderer, rvcara::ConversionModification |
| `src/insert/` | rvcara::InsertConverter |
| `src/ui/` | rvcara::PanelLookAndFeel, rvcara::PitchCurveView, rvcara::PitchTrack |

`dsp/`, `common/` and `model/` know nothing about ARA or plug-in formats, which is why the
tests link them directly without instantiating a plug-in.

@section building Building

Requires CMake 3.24+, a C++20 compiler, and the submodules. Two of them are superprojects
whose own contents are submodules.

@code
git submodule update --init --depth 1
git -C libs/ARA_SDK submodule update --init --depth 1 ARA_API ARA_Library
git -C libs/onnxruntime submodule update --init --depth 1 cmake/external/onnx

cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
cmake --build build
ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure
@endcode

VST3 and LV2 are built everywhere and AU on macOS; the format list follows the host operating
system, locally and in CI alike. Each artefact carries the ONNX Runtime shared library and its
soname chain, with `$ORIGIN` in the runpath so they are found beside it.

Every dependency is a submodule, ONNX Runtime included, so the commits this repository names
are the whole of what it runs. That costs:

- 25 to 45 minutes to compile ONNX Runtime, once, into `build/onnxruntime-install`.
- A **Python 3.10+ interpreter**, as ONNX Runtime's build tool: its CMake generates the export
  symbol list by running `tools/ci_build/gen_def.py`. Nothing in RVCARA needs Python and
  nothing runs at plug-in load.
- Network access at its configure step, which fetches abseil, protobuf, flatbuffers, re2,
  eigen and cpuinfo from `libs/onnxruntime/cmake/deps.txt`.
- About 6 GB of disk and, at ten parallel jobs, around 8 GB of memory.

`-DRVCARA_ONNXRUNTIME_ROOT=<path>` skips all of it and uses an existing installation.

The note segmentation model is a pretrained asset rather than source, so it cannot be a
submodule. The configure step downloads GAME's 45 MB small ONNX release into `res/models/GAME`
once, which `.gitignore` covers along with every other model. `-DRVCARA_FETCH_NOTE_MODEL=OFF`
stops it trying, and `-DRVCARA_NOTE_MODEL_URL=<url>` points it somewhere else. Without the
model everything still builds and converts; the editor reports that it found no notes.

On Linux, JUCE needs ALSA, FreeType, Fontconfig, X11 and GL development packages.

@section voices Voices

A voice is a directory under `res/models/<name>/` holding `synthesizer.onnx`, `retrieval.bin`
(the timbre codebook) and `manifest.json`.

The synthesiser is RVC v2's `SynthesizerTrnMs768NSFsid` whole: a text encoder over the content
features, a posterior encoder, a residual coupling flow, and only then `GeneratorNSF`, the NSF
HiFi-GAN that makes the samples. Calling the file a vocoder named its last stage after the
whole of it, so it is named for the model it is. A manifest that still says `vocoder` is read
as it always was.

Two of the graphs the pipeline runs are not the voice at all. The content encoder is ContentVec
and the pitch estimator is RMVPE; neither is fine-tuned per singer, and between them they are 740 MB of a voice's 1.2 GB. Each therefore gets a directory
of its own beside the voices, named for its model and laid out the way `GAME/` is:

@code
res/models/ContentVec/  config.json  contentvec.onnx
res/models/RMVPE/       config.json  rmvpe.onnx  mel_filter_bank.bin
@endcode

RVC ships the encoder as `hubert_base.pt`, which is a misnomer it inherited: the weights are
ContentVec, a HuBERT trained to leave the speaker out of its features, not Facebook's HuBERT.
The directory and the file are named for the model rather than for RVC's filename, and a loose
`hubert_base.onnx` beside the voices is still found.

A model that is not the voice describes itself rather than being described by every voice that
borrows it, which is what the `config.json` is for. rvcara::ContentEncoderManifest carries the
graph's two tensor names, the rate and frame rate it works at, and the width of the features it
returns. rvcara::PitchEstimatorManifest carries its tensor names, its whole mel front end — the
filter bank included, since which filter bank RMVPE wants is RMVPE's business — and the
constants its salience map is decoded with. What stays in the voice's manifest is what is
actually the voice: the vocoder, the codebook, the coarse pitch embedding it was trained with,
the chunking and the defaults.

rvcara::VoiceModel resolves each graph in turn: the voice's own copy, then the model's
directory beside the voices, then a loose file under the manifest's name or the upstream one.
An export that still ships its own copies loads exactly as it did, described by its own
manifest. One that does not picks up the shared graph and rvcara::ModelManifest::adopt takes
that model's word for what it is. Adopting an encoder whose features are the wrong width for
the voice's vocoder fails at load with the two widths named, and a graph found beside the
voices is checked against the input name it is about to be run with, so the wrong copy is
reported at load rather than at the first conversion.

rvcara::OnnxSession::getShared then keeps one session per resolved path for as long as any
voice is using it, so changing voice reloads the vocoder and the codebook and nothing else.
Keying on the path rather than on the role is what makes that safe: two voices share a session
only when they literally name the same file.

rvcara::VoiceModelLibrary searches, in increasing precedence: the shared application data
directory, the user's application data directory, this working copy's `res/models` (compiled
in at configure time, since a plug-in cannot reach the repository by a relative path), and any
path in `RVCARA_MODEL_PATH`. The same search finds the models that are not voices, which is how
`ContentVec/`, `RMVPE/` and `GAME/` are located; only directories carrying a `manifest.json`
are offered as voices, so none of the three ever appears in the selector.

Every pipeline constant lives in `manifest.json`, so a model trained at another sample rate or
feature width loads without a plug-in release. The ONNX tensor names are read from it too.

@section notes Notes

RVC conditions its vocoder on a fundamental track rather than on the source waveform's
partials, so the melody is an input the plug-in owns outright. Editing it is therefore just
editing that track before it reaches the vocoder, and the result is re-sung rather than
time-stretched or resampled.

To edit a melody by note, the take first has to be split into notes.
rvcara::NoteSegmenter does that with GAME, OpenVPI's Generative Adaptive MIDI Extractor: an
encoder embeds the waveform at one frame per ten milliseconds, a discrete diffusion segmenter
proposes the boundaries between notes and refines them over two passes, a third graph turns
those boundaries into durations, and an estimator names each note's pitch and says whether
anything was sung in it. A take longer than the encoder accepts is cut at its silences first.

The notes live on the audio modification, so they are per region and survive a session save.
Detection is a job of its own on a thread of its own, so notes arrive while the voice is still
converting rather than after it.

rvcara::EditedNote stores what the user did to a note as an offset from what was sung rather
than as an absolute pitch, together with a depth that says how much of the sung contour to keep
and a tilt for each edge. An untouched note therefore has an offset of zero at full depth and
no tilt, which rvcara::applyPitchEdit leaves bit for bit alone; a note that has been moved keeps
its own vibrato and scooping, moved bodily onto the new pitch. A note's centre is its median
rather than its mean, so a slide into it does not drag the answer along.

@section panel The panel

A header carrying the model selector, the pitch editor, and a footer reporting state. The
selector is the only control that changes what is rendered; the two scalers beside it scale
the view, not the audio.

The editor is laid out as a piano roll. A toolbar runs across the top, a keyboard column is
pinned to the left, a time ruler under the toolbar, and a waveform lane along the bottom;
between them the note grid scrolls on both axes, with one row per semitone from C2 to C6. Each
note becomes a block on the row it will be heard on, the melody the voice sang is drawn through
the blocks and the melody the take was sung at behind it, so vibrato, scooping and a note that
landed a semitone out are all visible against the row it should have sat on. The converted
waveform runs along the middle C line behind everything. The keyboard follows the vertical
scroll and the ruler and waveform the horizontal one, so each stays pinned to the axis it
labels. Opening a conversion centres the view on the sung range.

Dragging a block moves the note; without shift held it lands on the nearest degree of the
chosen scale, with shift it moves freely. Dragging where two blocks meet moves the boundary
between them, and dragging empty grid draws a band that selects what it touches. Shift or
command adds to a selection, and a tool given nothing selected acts on every note. Split cuts a
note where it is clicked and Glue joins a note to the one after it. Snap puts the selection on
the nearest degrees, Reset puts it back the way it was sung, and the scale button chooses what
"nearest" means — chromatic, or a major or minor key. Undo and redo cover every gesture, and
the arrow keys nudge by a semitone, or by a tenth of one with shift held.

A note selected on its own carries three controls above it, the way PitchNet's do. The outer
two tilt the note from its edges toward its middle, which is how a scoop into a note or a fall
out of it is put there or taken away; the middle one is the same shaping the Shape slider does,
dragged in place. All three are held rather than clicked, and all three are undoable.

The wheel scrolls the grid, and zooms when it is over the lane that labels the axis: over the
keyboard it zooms the pitch, over the ruler or the waveform it zooms the time, and in both
cases the point under the mouse is the point that stays still. Command and the wheel zoom
wherever the mouse is. While the host is playing, a line follows its transport through the
region on show.

An edit re-renders through the vocoder like any other setting, arriving progressively, so the
edited note is audible before the rest of the region has finished. Editing needs the notes and
the region, so it is offered under ARA only; loaded as an insert the editor still draws.

The panel's neutrals are warm and its one saturated colour is red, which is why a failure is
reported in amber: at a glance an alert has to look like something other than a selection.

There are no knobs. The parameters below still exist and the host can automate every one,
reached through the generic parameter view.

| Parameter | RVC name | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Pitch | `f0_up_key` | Transposes the estimated melody, in semitones |
| Timbre | `index_rate` | How far to move toward the trained singer's timbre |
| Consonants | `protect` | Keeps unvoiced frames nearer the source features |
| Dynamics | `rms_mix_rate` | Restores the source's loudness contour |
| Variation | — | Seeds the vocoder's latent; changes the performance, not the notes |
| Bypass | — | Automation only |

Consonant protection is inert for models whose pitch track is gap-filled, which is all of
them; it is kept because it matches upstream. See rvcara::PitchEstimator.

@section performance Performance

Measured stage by stage on a Ryzen 7 5800HS (8 cores) converting 37 seconds of vocal in
21.1 s, which is 0.57 times real time:

| Stage | Time | Share |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Synthesiser | 15.05 s | 71.3% |
| Content encoder | 3.31 s | 15.7% |
| Pitch estimator | 1.36 s | 6.4% |
| Resamplers, retrieval, filter | 1.40 s | 6.7% |

The synthesiser's 400-times upsampling to 40 kHz is the whole cost; making everything else
infinitely fast would buy 7%. ONNX Runtime's intra-op thread count is set to the number of
physical cores rather than its own default of hardware threads, which is 8% faster end to end.

Retrieval uses hnswlib rather than exact search: the codebook holds 61,893 vectors of 768
dimensions, and exact search is about 9.5 GFLOP per second of audio.

A warm voice load is 2 s; the first load of a voice is around 45 s, because the retrieval graph
is built and cached beside the model. Changing voice while another is already loaded costs less
than that again, since the content encoder and the pitch estimator stay where they are.

@section verification Verification status

- **Verified.** 51 GoogleTest cases pass. The exported assets match the reference
  implementation on real audio: pitch within 1.5 to 6.8 cents, correlation at least 0.999,
  log-mel distance 0.30 to 0.60, durations sample-exact. The DSP is checked against NumPy and
  SciPy through the fixtures in `tests/fixtures`, the mel spectrogram to 1e-3 and the
  zero-phase high-pass to 1e-5.
- **Verified end to end, outside a host.** Discovery, load and conversion of two sung phrases,
  compared against the reference render of the same files: log-spectrogram correlation 0.93 and
  0.91, against 0.64 and 0.71 for the untouched source.
- **Built and loads.** The VST3 builds with ARA enabled, carries its ARA main factory class,
  and dlopens cleanly.
- **Verified outside a host.** Note detection on real singing: a sung C major scale comes back
  as nineteen notes, rising then falling by the degrees of the scale, with rests at both ends,
  in 0.42 s for 3.4 s of audio; a 37 s aria comes back as 49 notes in 6.8 s, cut at its
  silences. Editing through the whole pipeline: a note moved by a fifth comes out of the
  estimator seven semitones higher on every frame with its contour intact, a note at zero depth
  comes out flat on its own median, and an edit nobody has touched returns the pitch track
  unchanged value for value.
- **Not yet verified.** Behaviour in a real host: ARA region editing, session persistence,
  insert-mode capture alignment against the timeline, and the pitch editor's gestures.

@section conventions Conventions

Names follow the framework and the field they belong to, in that order: JUCE house style for
C++ (`PascalCase` types and files, `camelCase` functions and variables, `num` for counts, no
member decoration, no `k` prefix), the originating API's spelling at a boundary (ARA entry
points, ONNX tensor names, manifest keys), and the published term over a paraphrase everywhere
else. Colours are named for their role rather than their hue, and `describe...` returns text
meant for a human.

The code carries no comments. Anything a name cannot say is documented here or as Doxygen on
the declaration.

Warnings come in two tiers. Every target gets `-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic` plus `-Wshadow`,
`-Wnon-virtual-dtor`, `-Wcast-align` and `-Woverloaded-virtual`, which JUCE's own sources pass.
A strict tier — `-Wconversion`, `-Wsign-conversion`, `-Wdouble-promotion`, `-Wfloat-conversion`,
`-Wold-style-cast`, `-Wuseless-cast`, `-Wcast-qual`, `-Wextra-semi` — goes on the translation
units that include no third-party header, which is why the DSP core is kept free of framework
headers. A flag judges every header a unit includes, and JUCE's contain 6,166 old-style casts
of their own; nothing here silences them, the flags are simply not applied to units that
include JUCE. Every source in this repository is clean under the full strict tier.

@section licence Licence

MIT. The submodules keep their own licences: JUCE is dual AGPL and commercial, the ARA SDK
has Celemony's terms, ONNX Runtime and googletest are MIT and BSD, hnswlib is Apache 2.0. GAME
is MIT and is downloaded rather than redistributed here. A voice carries the rights of the
recordings it was trained on.
*/
